## Core and React subscriptions

Use `graph.on(name, handler, options?)` for graph-wide events and `component.on(name, handler, options?)` for input on a scene component. Listeners receive one typed event with direct fields. For scene gestures, `target` identifies the hit component or graph background, `point.world` holds world coordinates and `event` is the native PointerEvent. Hover transitions have `target`, `relatedTarget`, `cause` and optional `sourceEvent`.

```ts
const unsubscribe = graph.on("click", ({ target, point }) => showDetails(target, point.world));
```

Call the returned disposer at the end of the subscription lifetime, or pass an `AbortSignal` in the options. React's `useGraphEvent` manages cleanup and receives the same event synchronously. GraphCanvas callback props such as `onPress`, `onDoubleClick` and `onBlocksSelectionChange` also receive the typed event.

```tsx
useGraphEvent(graph, "blocks-selection-change", ({ changes }) => updateSelection(changes));
```

## Custom layers

Subscribe in `afterInit()` with `this.onGraphEvent`. The subscription and its drag claims end when the layer detaches. Use `onCanvasEvent`, `onHtmlEvent` and `onRootEvent` for native DOM events on the layer's own surfaces. Keep scene gestures on the graph input route.

- Use `preventDefault()` to veto a gesture's default graph action. Use `stopPropagation()` to stop delivery along the scene route.
- Claim `dragstart` to own a scene drag. The input service tries the default component drag or camera pan after listeners have run.
- Observe proposed camera changes through `camera-change`. Subscribe to `graph.$camera` or override `Layer.onCameraChange()` for committed state.

See [Event model](../../packages/graph/docs/system/event-model.md), [Event subscriptions](../../packages/graph/docs/system/events.md) and [React hooks](../../packages/graph/docs/react/hooks.md).
